{
  "result": "The lighthouse stood at the edge of the harbor, where the water turned dark and the gulls cried before sunrise. Every morning, Mara climbed its narrow stairs with a thermos of tea and a notebook filled with half-finished poems. She liked the quiet before the town woke, the way the light swept across empty streets and sleeping boats. Below, fishermen mended nets, old radios crackled with weather reports, and the smell of salt and diesel drifted through the cold air. Mara was not the keeper; the light had been automated years ago. She was simply the person who remembered to visit, who believed places could feel forgotten if nobody noticed them. In her notebook she recorded small things: a red scarf caught on a railing, a child teaching a dog to swim, clouds shaped like maps of countries that did not exist. She thought memory was an act of kindness. One October morning, a storm took the pier and scattered the boats like toys. When the wind finally stopped, Mara helped carry buckets, sweep glass, and boil soup for neighbors whose kitchens had flooded. The lighthouse, improbably, kept turning. That night its beam found everyone working in the dark and made the ruined harbor look, briefly, held together. Years later, people would not remember the exact date of the storm. They would remember Mara's soup, the songs someone sang while hammering, and the steady light above them, reminding each tired person that morning was still coming, even then, for everyone.",
